Add Linting & Resolve linting warnings

The constant name cannot be SCREAMING_CAPS on the flutter.
example
```dart
// bad
const EXAMPLE_VALE = 1000

// bad
enum  ColorType{
  RED,
  BLUE,
}

// good
const exampleValue = 1000

// good
enum ColorType {
  red,
  blue,
}
```
![const_error](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/29420674/176372563-0791a658-005a-4d79-b414-379bb8e6df57.png)
![const_error2](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/29420674/176372571-f57eb5f2-d82e-4c5b-89e8-701222818edb.png)

See also: 
https://dart.dev/guides/language/effective-dart/style#prefer-using-lowercamelcase-for-constant-names

This PR is designed to address this issue #34

example:
```ts
// export { foo } from 'foo1';
// exports default 'foo';
// export { useB, _useC as useC };
// export function useA () { return 'a' }
// export { default } from "./other"
// export async function foo () {}
// export { foo as default }
//export * from "./other"
//export * as foo from "./other"

/**
 * export const a = 123
 * export { foo } from 'foo1';
 * exports default 'foo'
 * export function useA () { return 'a' }
 * export { useB, _useC as useC };
 *export { default } from "./other"
 *export async function foo () {}
 * export { foo as default }
 * export * from "./other"
 export * as foo from "./other"
 */

export { bar } from 'foo2';
export { foobar } from 'foo2';
```
The expected length of matches should be "2", but instead we get "17".

'\b' does not solve such problems, its semantics is the junction of words and non-words. Apparently the following code is also semantically correct.
```ts
// (\b is here)export { foo } from 'foo1';
```

**Solutions**：filter out comments before matching to avoid them affecting the matching process.

